.TH TOOLS9P 8
.SH NAME
tools9p \- serve a restricted Veltro tool and capability namespace
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tools9p
[
.B -DvN
]
[
.B -a
.I activity
]
[
.B -r
.I role
]
[
.B -m
.I mountpoint
]
[
.B -b
.I tool,...
]
[
.B -p
.I path[:ro|:rw]
]
.I tool ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tools9p
serves the active Veltro tools and their effective namespace capabilities at
.BR /tool .
Only named tools exist. Each invocation receives a namespace constructed by
.IR nsconstruct .
.PP
The effective paths file uses records of the form
.IR "path ro|rw|cow" .
Activity scratch is always
.B /tmp/veltro/scratch cow
and is constructed specially. Passing that path with
.B -p
does not recursively grant or stage it. Tools and ordinary path capabilities
are selected explicitly on the command line; child provisioning can only
narrow the declared delegation budget.
.PP
The control alias
.BR /mnt/toolctl
(for
.BI /tool. N\fR,
.BI /mnt/toolctl. N\fR)
is the same tree, outside every agent's name space, with three files a
tool call's
.B /tool
does not have:
.TP
.B ns
The name space a tool call gets, as
.IR ns (1)
prints it, made at the first read of an open by the restriction a call
uses, for a tool that adds no paths of its own.
.TP
.B event
A read waits for a change to the lists, then gives one word a line:
.B paths
after
.BR bindpath ,
.B unbindpath
or
.BR setperm ;
.B tools
after
.B add
or
.BR remove ;
.B budget
after
.B budget-add
or
.BR budget-remove .
Each open has its own queue: nothing is missed between reads.
.TP
.B view
The same name space, served: an open makes it, and what is written to
the file is 9P to it, so
.B mount -A /mnt/toolctl/view /n/agent
shows the agent's world as a tool call sees it.
It is read-only (a create, remove, wstat, write or open for writing is
refused), and it goes when the file is closed; a new mount shows changes
made since.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -D
Enable 9P protocol tracing.
.TP
.B -v
Enable verbose server logging.
.TP
.B -N
Accepted for compatibility and ignored.
.B /tool/meta/nodevs
is always
.BR set :
every tool runs with NODEVS applied
.RI ( exec
applies it in its own wrapper, after opening its wait file),
and a call whose NODEVS cannot be applied is refused.
.TP
.BI -a " activity"
Set the activity identity used for scratch and staged writes.
.TP
.BI -r " role"
Declare the audit role as
.B toplevel
or
.BR child .
.TP
.BI -m " mountpoint"
Serve somewhere other than
.BR /tool .
.TP
.BI -b " tool,..."
Set the maximum tool set that may be delegated to child tasks. Children can
narrow this set but cannot expand it.
.TP
.BI -p " path[:ro|:rw]"
Add an ordinary filesystem capability. Repeat as needed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.nf
	tools9p read list find grep
	tools9p -p /mnt/msg:ro -p /mnt/msg/draft:rw read write
	nsaudit -m /tool
	echo bindpath /usr/inferno ro > /mnt/toolctl/ctl
	mount -A /mnt/toolctl/view /n/agent; ls /n/agent/usr
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /tmp/veltro/.ns/manifest
Effective restricted namespace manifest, sealed into the audit chain when
auditing is enabled.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR nsaudit (1),
.IR namespace (6)
